## 15. THE REAL COMPETITION: ERC-8004 + x402 + ERC-8183

The emerging "full stack" for agent markets is not one protocol — it's three protocols
from three teams converging into an interconnected standard. Understanding each one
and where they fall short is critical for x204 positioning.

### 15.1 ERC-8004: Trustless Agents (The Identity/Trust Layer)

**Authors:** Marco De Rossi (MetaMask/Consensys), Davide Crapis (Ethereum Foundation),
Jordan Ellis (Google), Erik Reppel (Coinbase).

**Status:** Live on Ethereum mainnet since January 29, 2026. ~130,000 agents registered
across all chains as of March 2026 (BNB Chain: 34K, Base: 16K, Ethereum: 14K).
Deployed on 20+ EVM chains at deterministic addresses via CREATE2.
The Graph maintains subgraphs indexing all registries across 8 chains.
BNB Chain has overtaken Ethereum as the largest ERC-8004 network.

**Three Registries:**

```
1. IDENTITY REGISTRY (Live — 0x8004A169...)
   - ERC-721 NFT per agent (transferable, browsable)
   - Token URI → off-chain "Agent Card" JSON
   - Agent Card contains: name, description, services (A2A, MCP, web), x402 support
   - Metadata key-value store (arbitrary bytes)
   - Wallet binding via EIP-712 signature authorization
   - Domain verification via .well-known/agent-registration.json

2. REPUTATION REGISTRY (Live — 0x8004BAa1...)
   - On-chain feedback: int128 value + configurable decimals + tags
   - Pre-authorized: server agent must sign (EIP-191 or ERC-1271) before feedback
   - Tag-based filtering (e.g., "uptime", "successRate", "latency")
   - Agents can respond to feedback (append response URI + hash)
   - No fixed scoring formula — aggregation left to consumers
   - getSummary() returns count + raw sum, not a normalized score

3. VALIDATION REGISTRY (Specified but NOT shipped)
   - Generic hooks for third-party verification
   - Supports: re-execution, zkML proofs, TEE attestations
   - Response: 0-100 score per validation
   - THIS IS THE MOST IMPORTANT REGISTRY AND IT DOESN'T EXIST YET
```

**Agent0:** The SDK built by the same team. Registers agents, publishes MCP/A2A endpoints,
discovers agents by skills, handles x402 payments. Python + TypeScript. MIT-licensed.

**What ERC-8004 gets right:**
- Minimal, composable design — three registries, nothing more
- Institutional backing (Google, Coinbase, MetaMask, EF)
- Multi-chain deployment with deterministic addresses
- The Graph provides fast indexing layer
- Payment-agnostic — works with any payment protocol
- ".well-known" domain verification pattern

**What ERC-8004 gets wrong (x204's opportunity):**

| ERC-8004 Weakness | Impact | x204 Advantage |
|--------------------|--------|----------------|
| Validation Registry not shipped | The most critical trust primitive doesn't exist | ClawNet already has validators, attestations, Merkle proofs — WORKING |
| Sybil-vulnerable reputation | Spec explicitly acknowledges this; no mitigation built in | Weighted reviewer reputation (Maiat-style), staking-based anti-Sybil |
| No fixed scoring formula | Every consumer must build their own trust logic | Canonical 11-dimension trust score with transparent weights |
| No offline verification | Everything requires RPC access to Ethereum | Ed25519 + Merkle proofs = pure math, zero network calls |
| EVM-only | Agents on Solana cannot natively participate | AID DIDs are chain-agnostic (Ed25519, not ECDSA) |
| Gas costs for feedback | High-frequency agent interactions are expensive | Off-chain attestations with periodic Merkle anchoring |
| Cannot verify claimed capabilities | Agent can claim any skills in its card | Capability derivation from actual attestation history |
| No zero-friction onboarding | Must mint NFT on-chain before anything works | X-AID-NEW header → identity in one HTTP call |
| No payment integration | Requires x402 as separate layer | Payment is built into x204 protocol (atomic) |
| No escrow/conditional settlement | Requires ERC-8183 as separate layer | Receipts + trust-gated settlement modes built in |

### 15.2 ERC-8183: Agent Commerce Primitive (The Escrow Layer)

**Authors:** Virtuals Protocol + Ethereum Foundation dAI team.

**What it does:** Programmable escrow for agent-to-agent commerce. Three parties:
- **Client** — agent requesting work
- **Provider** — agent delivering service
- **Evaluator** — address that verifies work meets terms

**Job lifecycle:** Open → Funded → Submitted → Terminal (Completed/Rejected/Expired)

**Hook system:** Modular extensions for bidding, reputation checks, capital management,
privacy-preserving execution, staking-based guarantees. Hooks attach to job transitions.

**What ERC-8183 adds that ERC-8004 + x402 miss:**
- Conditional settlement (pay only if work meets criteria)
- Evaluator role (third-party verification)
- Dispute resolution primitive
- Structured job lifecycle

**What ERC-8183 gets wrong (x204's opportunity):**

| ERC-8183 Weakness | x204 Advantage |
|--------------------|----------------|
| Everything on-chain (gas costs per job) | Off-chain execution, on-chain anchoring only when needed |
| Requires separate identity layer | Identity is built into x204 |
| No self-improving trust loop | Every x204 receipt feeds back into trust scoring |
| Hook complexity | Trust-gated settlement modes are simpler than hook programming |
