## 16. HOW x204 IMPROVES UPON EIP-4361 AND EIP-712

### 16.1 EIP-4361: Sign-In with Ethereum (SIWE) — and Why It Fails for Agents

**What SIWE does:** Human-readable message signing for dApp authentication.

```
clawnet.org wants you to sign in with your Ethereum account:
0xABC...
Sign in to ClawNet marketplace
URI: https://clawnet.org
Version: 1
Chain ID: 8453
Nonce: abc123
Issued At: 2026-03-21T14:30:00Z
```

**SIWE's fundamental assumption:** A human is present to review and approve the message.

**Why SIWE breaks for AI agents:**

| SIWE Limitation | Impact on Agents | x204 Solution |
|-----------------|------------------|---------------|
| Human-in-the-loop | Agent has no "user" to approve signing | Agent holds Ed25519 key, signs autonomously |
| Session-oriented | "I am this address" — no scoping | x204 encodes: identity + budget + capabilities + expiry |
| No delegation model | Can't say "Agent X acts for Principal Y with budget Z" | AID DID hierarchy with trust chain inheritance |
| No agent-to-agent trust | Client-to-server only | Mutual authentication — both parties prove identity |
| No revocation | Once key is given, agent signs forever | Key rotation via `POST /:did/rotate-key`, audit trail |
| Single-chain binding | Each message bound to one chain ID | AID DIDs are chain-agnostic (Ed25519, not ECDSA) |
| No credential portability | Session on Service A means nothing to Service B | Portable AID documents verifiable anywhere offline |
| No reputation | Proves identity only, not trustworthiness | Trust score travels with every request |
| Text-based format | "wants you to sign in" — wasteful for machines | Binary/JSON headers optimized for agent-to-agent |

**x204's identity model vs SIWE:**
```
SIWE:  "I am address 0xABC on chain 8453" (session, no context)

x204:  "I am did:clawnet:zABC with trust score 87,
        1,247 successful transactions, Merkle-anchored,
        authorized for data_query and skill_invoke,
        budget 50 USDC, expires 2026-03-22T14:30:00Z,
        delegated from did:clawnet:zPRINCIPAL"
```

SIWE proves you ARE someone. x204 proves you are someone WORTH TRUSTING.

### 16.2 EIP-712: Typed Structured Data — and How x204 Extends It

**What EIP-712 does:** Deterministic hashing and signing of structured data.
```
sign(keccak256("\x19\x01" || domainSeparator || hashStruct(message)))
```

**How x402 uses EIP-712:** Signs EIP-3009 transfer authorizations (from, to, value, nonce).
The facilitator verifies the signature and submits the on-chain transfer.

**EIP-712's limitations for agent commerce:**

| EIP-712 Limitation | Impact | x204 Extension |
|--------------------|--------|----------------|
| No identity semantics | "This key signed this" — not WHO signed it | AID DID embedded in every signed message |
| Single-signer model | No native dual-signing | Payer + provider both sign every receipt |
| No negotiation semantics | Can encode results, not the negotiation | Trust-gated pricing tiers replace negotiation |
| No replay protection built-in | Apps must build their own nonces | Receipt IDs + Merkle anchoring = inherent replay protection |
| No conditional execution | All-or-nothing signatures | Trust-gated settlement: immediate/batched/deferred |
| EVM-only | Ethereum signing standard | Ed25519 (works everywhere: EVM, Solana, off-chain) |
| Human-display oriented | Wallet shows data for human review | Machine-optimized JSON headers |

**x204's signing model — EIP-712 + Ed25519 dual layer:**

```
Layer 1: EIP-712 (Payment)
  Agent signs EIP-3009 transferWithAuthorization via ECDSA (secp256k1)
  → Handles USDC settlement on Base L2
  → x402-compatible — any x402 server can verify the payment

Layer 2: Ed25519 (Identity + Trust)
  Agent signs request body with Ed25519 private key
  → Proves DID ownership (self-certifying)
  → Carries trust score, capabilities, Merkle proof
  → Verifiable offline — no RPC needed
  → Works across chains (not bound to EVM)

Combined in x204:
  PAYMENT-SIGNATURE: <EIP-712/EIP-3009 signed auth>     ← EVM payment
  X-AID-PROOF:       <Ed25519 sig over request body>      ← Universal identity
```

This dual-layer approach means x204 is BACKWARDS-COMPATIBLE with x402 (the PAYMENT-SIGNATURE
header works with any x402 facilitator) while ADDING identity, trust, and offline verification
through the Ed25519 layer.

