## 20. SECURITY MODEL

### 20.1 Threat Analysis

| Threat | Vector | Mitigation |
|--------|--------|------------|
| DID spoofing | Attacker claims another agent's DID | X-AID-PROOF: Ed25519 signature over request body proves key ownership. Without the private key, signature verification fails. |
| Trust score manipulation | Sybil feedback from fake agents | Weighted feedback (0.5x-5x based on reporter history). Creating high-weight agents costs real money. |
| Replay attacks | Resubmit old X-AID-PROOF | Signature includes request body hash + timestamp. Server rejects proofs older than 5 minutes. Receipt IDs are unique (nanoid). |
| Man-in-the-middle | Intercept and modify request/response | HTTPS required (enforced). Ed25519 signatures cover the full request body — any modification invalidates the signature. |
| Settlement fraud (deferred) | Agent consumes service, disappears before paying | Credit limits based on trust score. Agents must sign EIP-3009 authorization upfront with daily cap. Server holds the signed auth. |
| Key compromise | Attacker steals Ed25519 private key | Key rotation via `POST /:did/rotate-key`. Old key marked as rotated with audit trail. Revocation propagates to trust snapshots. |
| Private key exposure | privateKeySeed returned in HTTP response | HTTPS required. Seed shown once, never stored server-side. Response includes explicit warning. Client must persist the seed. |
| Stale trust data | Cross-platform verification uses outdated score | AID documents have `issuance.expiresAt` (90 days). Verifiers check expiry. Trust snapshots are timestamped. |
| Gas griefing | Attacker triggers many deferred settlements | Per-agent batch limits. Minimum settlement amount (0.01 USDC). Server batches settlements, not one tx per receipt. |
| Evaluator collusion (ERC-8183 equivalent) | In x204, the "evaluator" is the receipt system | Receipts are dual-signed (payer + provider). Either party can dispute. Merkle anchoring makes tampering detectable. |

### 20.2 Required Security Properties

```
1. HTTPS REQUIRED — all x204 endpoints MUST be served over TLS
2. TIMESTAMP VALIDATION — X-AID-PROOF must include current timestamp (±5 min)
3. BODY BINDING — Ed25519 signature covers SHA-256 of request body
4. NONCE TRACKING — Receipt IDs are unique; duplicate submissions rejected
5. KEY ROTATION — compromised keys can be rotated without losing identity
6. CREDIT LIMITS — deferred settlement capped by trust score
7. RATE LIMITING — public endpoints rate-limited by IP (300/hr)
8. AUDIT TRAIL — all AID operations logged via logAudit()
```

